      "textEn": "223\n\nCannot dare to kill. Jarat Bharat was a great friend of all living beings. They were not enemies of anyone.\nAnd they always remained absorbed in the meditation of the complete Supreme Personality of Godhead. He was born in the lineage of an excellent Brahmin.\nTherefore, even if he were an enemy or aggressive to someone, his killing\nwas prohibited. In no way was there any justification for killing the root Bharat, therefore this\ncould not be tolerated by Bhadrakali. They immediately understood that these sinful dacoits are going to kill a supreme devotee of God. Therefore, the body of the Sastha idol was torn apart, and the goddess Kali herself\nappeared. Their body was burning with intense and unbearable heat. Note: Only the aggressor can be killed according to Vedic injunctions. If someone comes with the intention of killing,\nthen he can be immediately killed for self-defense. It has also been said that if\nsomeone comes to set fire to a house or to defile or abduct someone's wife, then he\ncan be killed.\nLord Ramachandra killed all of Ravana's family because Ravana had\nabducted his wife, Sita Devi. But in the scriptures, there is no command for killing for any other purpose. Those who are the expansions of God on the gods, those people are allowed to sacrifice animals\nwho eat meat. It is a kind of prohibition on meat. In other words, in the Vedas, there is a prohibition on the slaughter of animals\nthrough certain rules. Considering these things, it can be said that there was no justification for killing the root Bharat, who was born in a high\nBrahmin family. They were self-realized and were the well-wishers of all beings. According to the Vedas, the acceptance of the killing of Jarat Bharat by thieves and high-caste people\nis not obtained. Thus Goddess Bhadrakali emerged from the idol to protect the devotees of Ishwar. Srila\nVishwanath Chakravarti Thakur explains in this way that the divine radiance of the inert Bharat was such that the idol\nwas torn apart.\nThose who are intoxicated by passion and darkness, thieves, and arrogance, and those who are intoxicated by wealth, offer human sacrifices before the Goddess Kali. It is not permitted by the Vedas. At this time, there are thousands of slaughterhouses all over the world,\nwhich are run by people who are crazy about luxury. Such acts by the Bhagwat Sampradaya\nare never approved."
