      "textEn": "227\n\nIf one neglects, then he is punished by Lord Shiva's trident. Srisuka said,\n\nThen, the king of Sindhu, the lord of the forest, went to the grove of sugarcane, to the place of the lord of the clan, at the time of seeking the man who carries the palanquin.\nBy the grace of God, this Brahmin was available. He drank this young man, the one with the strong gourd, the one with the broken pot, thus he was taken to the house.\nHe was taken along, and the great one was brought to the palanquin. 1>. Meaning of the word.\n\nShri-Shuka said—Shukadeva Goswami spoke; Ath—in this way; Sindhu-Sauvira-Pateha—of the ruler of the Sindhu and Sauvira kingdoms;\nRahu-Ganasya—of the king named Rahugan; Vrajatah—going to (Kapil's) ashram; Ikshu-Matyaah Tateha—on the banks of Ikshumati; Tat—that—\nKulapatina—by the chief of the palanquin carriers; Shibika-Vaha—to become a palanquin bearer; Purusha-Anveshan-Samaye—while searching for a man;\nDaivena—by good fortune; Upasaditah—having gone near; Sah—He; Dwija-Varaha—the Brahmin's son, Jad Bharat;\nAvailable—Obtained; Eshah—This man; Piva—Extremely strong; Yuva—Young; Sanhanan-Angah—With well-formed limbs; Go-Khar-\nLike—like a bull or donkey; Dhuram—burden, load; Vodhum—to carry; Alam—capable; Iti—thinking thus;\nPurva-vistiti-grahitaiha—Forcefully made to work, caught in bondage; Saha—together; Grihitah—Captured; Prasabham—\nForcefully; A-tat-Arhah—Even though unable to carry a palanquin; Uvah—was carrying; Shibikam—Palanquin; Saha—He; Maha-\nAnubhabah—Great man..\n\nShukdev Goswami further said, O King, after this, the ruler of the Sindhu and Sauvira regions\nRahugan was going to Kapilashram. When the king's chief palanquin bearers (palanquin carriers) reached the banks of Ikshumati,\nthey needed another palanquin bearer. Therefore, they started searching for such a person,\nand by chance, they found Jarat Bharat. He thought that this is young and strong and\nIts limbs are strong. This is very suitable for carrying loads equivalent to oxen and donkeys. Thinking this, although Mahatma Jad Bharat was completely unsuitable for such a task, still the Kaharas\nForcibly compelled him to carry the palanquin without hesitation.\nWhen the wise men are not aware of the right path, then the path of the unwise is difficult. The wise men are not afraid of the path of the unwise.\nThe wise men are not afraid of the path of the unwise, why is it difficult to understand? 2>. Meaning of the word\n\nYada—when; Hi—certainly; Dwija-varasya—of the root Bharat; Ishu-matra—one arrow ahead (3 feet); Avaloka-anugatah—\nBy following by looking; Na samahita—not matching; Purusha-gatih—of the men; Tada—At that time; Vishama-gatam—\nBeing uneven; Swa-shi-bikam—one's own palanquin; Rahuganah—the king Rahugan; Upadharya—understanding; Purushan—men\nFrom; Adhvahata—carrying the palanquin; Aah—said; Hee—Oh; Vodharah—of the king; Sadhu atikramat—one who walks equally so that\nThere is no jumping; Kimiti—For what reason; Vishama—unbalanced, High-low; Uhyate—is being taken; Yanam—palanquin;\nIti—in this way.But because of their non-violent nature, they were not taking the palanquin properly."
