      "textEn": "240\n\n“Oh God! He who constantly waits to receive your unselfish grace and to you,\nWhile respectfully bowing from the heart, continues to suffer the consequences of past misdeeds, he is certainly a part of liberation\nbecause it becomes his right.\"\n\nSa chapi pandaveya sindhusauvirapati tatvajnanasyaamyakshraddhayadhikrutadhikrityastadhridayagranthimochanam\nDwijavacha ashutya bahuyoggranthasamatam tvarayaruhya shirasam padamulamupasrithah kshamapayanvigatanrupadvesmay\nUvacha. 1. Meaning of the word\n\nSaha - He (Maharaj Rahugan); Cha - also; Api - even; Panduveya - He who is the best of the Pandava lineage (Maharaj Parikshit); Sindhu-Sauvira - He who is the king of the kingdoms named Sindhu and Sauvira; Sapta-Saha - He who is the seventh among the seven; Tatva-Jnanaaya - about the knowledge of the ultimate truth;\nSamyak-Shraddhaya - with complete control over the senses and mind with faith; Adhikrita - Authorized; Samayukta - Accomplished;\nTat - that; Hruday-granthi - a knot of false thoughts inside the heart; Mochanam - the one who opens; Dwija-vachah - of a Brahmin (Root Bharat);\nShabd - word; Ashrutya - having heard; Bahu-yoga-granthah - agreeable to all yogas and their texts; Tvaraya - quickly; Avaruhya - descending (from the palanquin); Shirsa - with one's head; Pad-moolam - on the lotus feet; Upasrithah - having prostrated oneself; Kshamapayan - having obtained forgiveness for one's offense; Vigat-nrip-deva-smayah - having abandoned the false pride of being a king;\nUvacha - said. Shukdev Goswami further said—Oh, best of the Pandava dynasty (Maharaj Parikshit), the king of Sindhu and Sauvira\nhad faith in the discussions of the ultimate truth. In this way, due to being worthy,\nHe heard that philosophical teaching from Jad Bharat, which all the books of yoga practice recommend,\nand from which the knot in the heart loosens. Thus ends his reign of whirlwind proportions.\nHe\nimmediately came down from the palanquin and, placing his head at the lotus feet of the root Bharat, fell prostrate on the earth\nso that he could receive forgiveness for the insultful words spoken to this Brahmin. Then he prayed like this. Meaning: In the Bhagavad Gita (4.2), Lord Krishna says—\n\nAnd this tradition has been passed down by the best of kings. By the blackness, the great yoga is destroyed, O scorcher of enemies. “Thus, this supreme science was obtained through the Guru-Parampara (teachings of a Guru), and the Rajarshis (kings) knew it through this very method. But chronologically, that tradition broke, due to which this science seems to have become endangered.\nIt seems.”"
